<worksheet xml:id="ws-fields-activity">

	<title>Module 1: Fields</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>The Good Place</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Sets of Numbers (and one set of matrices)</title>
		
    <p>
        We'll start with some formalizing of things you have likely seen before:
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>natural numbers</term> is
                    <md>
                        \mathbb{N}= \{1,2,3,\dots\}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>whole numbers</term> is
                    <md>
                        \mathbb{W}= \{0,1,2,3,\dots\}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>integers</term> is all positive and
                    negative natural numbers, as well as 0:
                    <md>
                        \mathbb{Z}= \{\dots,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,\dots\}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>rational numbers</term> is
                    <md>
                        \mathbb{Q}= \left\{\frac{a}{b}: a,b \in \mathbb{Z}, b\neq 0\right\}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>real numbers</term>, denoted
                    <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is something you likely have an intuitive
                    idea about.
                    Defining <m>\mathbb{R}</m> rigorously is actually quite
                    difficult, and can occupy a significant amount of time in a
                    first course in real analysis.
                    So we'll make use of your intuition in this class.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>irrational numbers</term> is
                    <m>\mathbb{R}\setminus \mathbb{Q}</m>.
                    That is, the set of real numbers that are not rational.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of <term>complex numbers</term> is
                    <md>
                        \mathbb{C}= \{a+bi: a,b\in\mathbb{R}\}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The set of
                    <term> <m>2\times 2</m> matrices with real entries</term>
                    is
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{M}_{2}(\mathbb{R}) = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix}a &amp;b \\ c &amp;d\end{bmatrix}: a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{R}\right\}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>

<!-- Division? -->
<!--    <title>Binary Operations</title> -->

	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Binary Operations</title>
    <p>
        Our English word algebra comes from the Arabic word al-jabr, which means
        <q>the reunion of broken parts</q>.
        It is not surprising, then, that we will be considering ways of
        combining two elements of a set into one; to do this, we turn to a
        particular type of function, known as a binary operation.
        Let <m>X</m> be a nonempty set.
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A function <m>\star: X\times X \to Y</m> is called a
                    <alert>binary operation on <m>X</m> </alert>.
                    That is, the function <m>\star</m> takes two numbers in the
                    set <m>X</m>, and outputs another number, potentially in a
                    different set <m>Y</m>.
                    Given <m>a,b\in X</m>, we usually write <m>a\star b</m> in
                    place of the typical function notation <m>\star(a,b)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>\star</m> is a binary operation on <m>X</m> such that
                    <m>\star:X\times X \to X</m> we say that <m>X</m> is closed
                    under the operation <m>\star</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <example>
        <title>Ordered pairs</title>
        <p>Say <m>X = \mathbb{Z}</m>.
        Let <m>\cdot</m> be the binary operation multiplication.
        Then, with the function notation, we could write <m>\cdot(2,3) = 6</m>,
        though we usually write <m>2\cdot 3=6</m>.
        Since, whenever <m>x,y \in \mathbb{Z}</m> we have
        <m>x\cdot y \in \mathbb{Z}</m>, we can say
        <m>\cdot: X\times X \to X</m>.
        Thus, <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> is closed under the operation <m>\cdot</m>.
        However, <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> is not closed under the binary operation
        <m>\div</m> because, for example,
        <m>\div(2,3)= 2\div 3 = \frac{2}{3}\not\in\mathbb{Z}</m>.</p>
    </example>
    
    </paragraphs>

	
	<paragraphs>
    <title>Fields</title>


    <p>
        A <term>field</term> is a set <m>F</m> together with two binary
        operations, called addition ( <m>+</m> ) and multiplication (<m>\cdot</m> ), denoted as the triple <m>(F, +,\cdot)</m>, such that all
        of the following axioms hold:
    </p>

    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The set <m>F</m> is
                    <alert>closed under addition and multiplication</alert>,
                    meaning that for all <m>x,y\in F, x+y \in F</m> and
                    <m>x\cdot y \in F</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Addition and multiplication are associative</alert>,
                    meaning that for all <m>x,y,z\in F</m>,
                    <m>(x+y)+z = x+(y+z)</m>, and
                    <m>(x\cdot y)\cdot z = x\cdot(y\cdot z)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Addition and multiplication are commutative</alert>,
                    that is, for all <m>x,y\in F</m>, <m>x+y = y+x</m> and
                    <m>x\cdot y = y\cdot x</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>The set <m>F</m> contains an additive identity</alert>.
                    That is, there exists an element <m>0_{F} \in F</m> such
                    that <m>a+0_{F} = a</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>The set <m>F</m> contains a multiplicative identity</alert>.
                    That is, there exists an element <m>1_{F} \in F</m> such
                    that for all <m>a\in F</m>, <m>1_{F} \cdot a = a</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Every element of <m>F</m> has an additive inverse within <m>F</m> </alert>,
                    meaning that for every <m>x\in F</m>, there exists a
                    <m>y\in F</m> such that <m>x+y = 0_{F}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Every nonzero (i.e., not <m>0_{F}</m>) element of <m>F</m> has a multiplicative inverse within <m>F</m> </alert>,
                    meaning that for every <m>x\in F</m>, there exists a
                    <m>y\in F</m> such that <m>x\cdot y =1_{F}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Multiplication distributes over addition</alert>,
                    meaning that, for all <m>x,y,z \in F</m>,
                    <m>x\cdot(y+z) = x\cdot y + x\cdot z</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
	<exercise workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>For one of the sets on page 1, determine which
        of the 8 properties of a field it has and which it doesn't (assume the
        addition and multiplication you've been taught in the past for each
        set).
        Is the set a field?</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
</page>


<page>
	<paragraphs>
    <title>Polynomials</title>

        <p>A good deal of time is spent studying polynomials in algebra courses
        that are intended to be a preparation for the study of calculus.
        A <alert>polynomial in <m>x</m> over <m>\mathbb{R}</m> </alert> is an
        expression of the form
        <md>
            a_{n} x^{n} + a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots + a_{2}x^{2} + a_{1} x^{1} + a_{0} x^{0}.
        </md>
        The set of all polynomials in <m>x</m> over <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is denoted
        <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>.
        We'll use the addition and multiplication you likely learned in high
        school (in addition, you <q>add like terms</q>, in multiplication you need
        to use the distributive property).</p>

    <exercise workspace="4in">
    	<statement>
        <p>Which of the properties of a field do polynomials have?</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
	</paragraphs>
	
	<paragraphs>
    <title>Other Operations</title>

    <exercise workspace="4in">
        <statement>Consider <m>(\mathbb{R}, -,\div)</m>.
        That is, the real numbers, but in the field axioms, replace addition
        with subtraction, and multiplication with division.
        Is <m>(\mathbb{R}, -,\div)</m> a field?</statement>
    </exercise>
    
	</paragraphs>
	
</page>

<page xml:id="ws-rectangle-math">
	<paragraphs>
    <title>Rectangle Math</title>

    <p>
        This definition of multiplication is taken from James Tanton (who is
        math YouTube famous).
    </p>

    <p>
        Here's how it works: To compute the product of two numbers, say four
        times three, draw a four-by-three array of dots and count the number of
        (horizontal/vertical) rectangles one can draw with vertices on the grid.
        (Squares are also considered rectangles.)
    </p>

    <p><image source="images/Fields-RectangleMath.png" width="25%" /></p>

    <p>
        Here, there are six <m>1\times 1</m> rectangles, three <m>1\times 2</m>
        s, four <m>2\times 1</m> s, two <m>2\times 2</m> s, two <m>3\times 1</m>
        s, and one <m>3\times 4</m>, giving a total of eighteen rectangles.
        So,
        <md>
            4\times_{\square} 3 = 18.
        </md>
        Here the <m>\times_{\square}</m> is standing for <q>rectangle multiplication</q>.
    </p>
        
    <exercise workspace="3in">
    <p>Your goal is to figure out if the properties of a field hold with this
        new multiplication.
        But wait!
        You also need a set and an addition.
        Let's decide our set is the whole numbers, <m>\mathbb{W}</m> and the
        addition is the standard addition you know and love.
        I recommend you start by computing rectangle multiplication for a few
        pairs of whole numbers!</p>
    </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
</page>

